{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED — independent review of exact head 263068a79cde12171e015d397c0753852856b2b9 on request/succession-staleness, for review request f6878966 under governing request be558440, co-signing all four named artifacts: internal/workroom 5b673fdc0edaf6659171d74fd89486c2bfade9ec (primary), internal/docset dd2dac1d, docs 2ae7a20d and ui 75329c54.\n\nCODEX'S FINDING a9564eb5 IS CLOSED, and I checked each half of it. Succession now carries the link rather than a boolean: succeededRetirements returns a predecessor-to-successor map, so staleness can follow the chain instead of stopping at the first hop. successionCondemned walks that chain to its end and reports the terminal artifact's retirement, with a visited set that returns false on a cycle — the conservative direction, since a cycle declines to condemn rather than flaring on a malformed chain. A successor replaced by a further successor keeps answering; a terminal successor retired with no successor of its own condemns the predecessor, and what stood on it is told exactly as if its own basis had been retired. The merge receipt is included through withoutCondemnedSuccessions, so a signed plan stops covering a condemned chain.\n\nMUTATION. Forcing successionCondemned to return false fails TestCondemnedSuccessorFlaresWhatStoodOnThePredecessor, and nothing else. The condemnation path is pinned by exactly the test the request named, and the worktree restored clean.\n\nTHE RULE NOW HAS ONE DEFINITION, which is the real simplification here. internal/docset/succession.go and its test are deleted, and the two gate call sites read artifact.Succeeded from the projection instead of recomputing the rule locally. I compared the two implementations before accepting the deletion: pathCovers is preserved in the fold with identical semantics — exact string, or a directory containing the predecessor, compared slash-delimited — so the docset gate classifies citations the same way it did, now from the fold's answer rather than a parallel copy of the reasoning. Two implementations of one rule is precisely the shape that drifts, and this removes the second.\n\nSECURITY, AND THE CHOICE I MOST WANTED TO CHECK. Succession is established only by the retiring act's own signed rests_on naming an artifact at the same path or a covering directory. The code rejects the structural shortcut explicitly, and the comment names the failure it would cause: an artifact retired because its claim was never true would be quietly rescued by the next unrelated publication in that tree, and everything resting on the false claim would stop flaring. Since a bystander cannot supply the signed basis, forging succession needs the retiring actor's own signature, which is the same authority the retirement already required. That is the right boundary, and it is the difference between a pointer that moved and a claim that was withdrawn. No unresolved security defect.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Four maintained paths move together and each carries its artifact at this head. internal/workroom changes the fold contract — Artifact.Succeeded is added and the schema version moves to workroom-fold@6 — and internal/docset loses a surface it no longer owns. The contract change is documented in the same head: docs/concepts/staleness.md, docs/reference/architecture.md and docs/reference/gs/status.md all state the distinction between a succeeded retirement and a condemnation, and ui/src/lib/api.ts mirrors the new field so the interface reads what the fold now publishes. Documented together with the behaviour, as required.\n\nGATES, INDEPENDENTLY RUN at the exact head in my own worktree: gofmt reports nothing, go vet is clean, git diff --check is clean, go test -count=1 ./... passes 30 packages with zero failures, go test -race is clean for internal/workroom, internal/wireparity and internal/statusview, and make ui-check exits 0 leaving the tree clean, so the committed embed matches ui/src.\n\nONE FACT ABOUT THE BASE, recorded rather than held against the head. This branch was cut from d6843b48 and main has since moved to eb7d2dfd, so it does not fast-forward; the request says so itself. It merges cleanly — git merge-tree against current main returns no conflict — and the branch's own three-dot diff is confined to the twelve files above. A two-dot diff against main will show files main gained since as deletions; that is base drift, not a revert, and whoever merges should read three-dot.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Already taken: this head deletes 152 lines while adding the behaviour, and the deletion is the duplicate rule rather than coverage. Nothing further to cut.\n\nNo change to this head is required. Any change to it invalidates this approval.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5b673fdc0edaf6659171d74fd89486c2bfade9ec","head":"263068a79cde12171e015d397c0753852856b2b9","verdict":"approved"}}